    "readout_answer": "7",
    "readout_text": "To solve the problem, we begin by analyzing the total amount of orange juice and how it is distributed among the four glasses.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the Total Amount of Juice\n\nEach glass has the same capacity, and we assume the capacity of one glass is 1 unit. \n\n- The first three glasses are completely filled:  \n  $$\n  1 + 1 + 1 = 3 \\text{ units}\n  $$\n\n- The fourth glass is only filled to $\\frac{1}{3}$ of its capacity:  \n  $$\n  \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ units}\n  $$\n\nSo, the total amount of juice is:\n$$\n3 + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{10}{3} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Equal Amount per Glass\n\nTo make all four glasses have the same amount of juice, we divide the total amount by 4:\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3} \\div 4 = \\frac{10}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{10}{12} = \\frac{5}{6} \\text{ units per glass}\n$$\n\nSo, each glass should end up with $\\frac{5}{6}$ units of juice.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine the Amount to be Added to the Fourth Glass\n\nThe fourth glass currently has $\\frac{1}{3}$ units. To reach $\\frac{5}{6}$, it needs:\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{2}$ unit of juice must be added to the fourth glass.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Determine the Amount to Be Taken from Each of the First Three Glasses\n\nSince the juice must be taken **equally** from the first three glasses, and the total amount to be transferred is $\\frac{1}{2}$, we divide this among the three glasses:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{6} \\text{ units from each of the first three glasses}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Final Answer\n\nThe fraction of a glass that must be poured from each of the first three glasses into the fourth is:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThis is already in lowest terms, so $m = 1$, $n = 6$, and:\n$$\nm + n = 1 + 6 = \\boxed{7}\n$$",
